  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through Monday)\nIssued at 123 AM CDT Tue Aug 4 2026\n\nA hot and mostly rain free forecast remains persistent through the \nrest of the workweek and the upcoming weekend. Mid-level ridging \ncentered across the Desert Southwest is expected to bridge through \nTexas as it connects with another ridge developing across Florida \nthat would extend into the Gulf. The low-level moisture across the \nregion, especially along and east of the I-35 corridor, slightly \ndeepens as we approach the end of the work week and during the \nupcoming weekend with the persistent light to moderate south and \nsoutheasterly onshore flow. The moisture and wind pattern is enough \nto where we'll highlight a low (10-25%) chance for afternoon or \nearly evening sea breeze convection that may try to enter our \nsoutheastern most Coastal Plain counties, beginning Thursday. Rain \nfree conditions primarily prevail elsewhere during the long term \nperiod. Seasonable heat continues across the region but afternoon \nhighs may shave off a few more degrees as the low-level moisture \nbecomes slightly deeper. However, expect for those heat indices to \nnot budge at all and will remain elevated close to or just shy of \nthe heat advisory criteria. Don't forget your heat safety. Warm \novernight lows, generally in the mid 70s, will continue with the \ndevelopment of low stratus from late night into each morning.",
